>>>
>>> So, here are the issues:
>>>
>>> To be useful, P?S/8 needs a string of logical units setup differently 
>>> from each other to serve different purposes.  It is in fact trivial to set 
>>> these different units up, but it is not documented to my satisfaction ]but 
>>> I am slowly writing adequate documentation so that a knowledgeable user can 
>>> know what to do.. Because of other issues, making such utilities automated 
>>> in the general sense is a low priority,, however, there are stopgap 
>>> measures to write.
>>>
>>> I will explain the above with an example.
>>>
>>> Unlike OS/8, P?S/8 actually has "2.x" directories currently [and in 
>>> future versions that will become 3.x]..  Here is the purpose of the 
>>> directories [and I have written schematic level documents to explain the 
>>> details].
>>>
>>> 1) The System Programs Directory.
>>>
>>> This is where conforming system programs are stored.  There are several 
>>> parameters that each program has to have specified to be totally practical, 
>>> due to the nature of P?S/8, [A rough comparison here is that the innards of 
>>> OS/8 CCL needs to set certain things for each program. In the P?S/8 
>>> alliterative, they are set in the entry within the System Programs 
>>> Directory.
>>>
>>> Again,, there is no utility to just trivially make a system program.  
>>> You cannot make an analogy to OS/8 because a P?S/8 system program has 
>>> powerful features that can be availed of where there is no OS/8 analog. In 
>>> general, they cause efficient execution or other features.
>>>
>>> Here are a few of the parameters.
>>>
>>> 1) This program demands the parsing of any optional =*xxxx* parameter 
>>> be be done in the current radix [which the user can set to decimal by 
>>> default or possibly octal, but generally not the case].  This invariably 
>>> means decimal, but it more properly works as I described here.  The reason 
>>> for this is for commands such as FIND "STRING" or CHANGE 
>>> "STRING"REPLACEMENT STRING which are used as transient overlays to the 
>>> keyboard monitor and integrated editor for obvious reasons as the user 
>>> edits files.
>>>
>>> 2) Inhibit parsing of parameters entirely.  P?S/8 does not use a 
>>> command-decoder; the commands essentially resemble a subset of OS/8 CCL 
>>> without the overhead which is profound on tape-based systems such as TC08 
>>> and TD8E and TC12.  The commands are powerful yet lack the overhead.
>>>
>>> In essence, you get all of what OS/8 asks to get a program going and 
>>> also pass files to it, but all in one line always.
>>>
>>> The OS/8 PAL command is essentially identical [except the command-line 
>>> switch options of P?S/8 PAL are a) particularly different and b) there are 
>>> more of them; in fact there are 37 of the 36 switches implemented; one of 
>>> them has two distinct meanings that are not conflicting].  A contrived 
>>> example could use the identical commands for both systems, but the options 
>>> quickly diverge.
>>>
>>> The "flow" of commands in OS/8 is always output < input.  Some people 
>>> prefer input > output.  P?S/8 actually works both ways.
>>>
>>> For the benefit of programs such as SET that takes parameters and 
>>> sub-parameters [some partial overlap with OS/8] it is necessary to prevent 
>>> all potential file parsing.
>>>
>>> 3) Inhibit only input file parsing but allow output file parsing.  This 
>>> is a one-off at present for the BSAVE command.  BSAVE can save arbitrary 
>>> groupings of memory in 32K, even down to individual memory locations.  This 
>>> is because the output is to P?S/8 "slurp" binary files, not core images.  
>>> These can later be loaded with the "slurp" loader as any other binary file, 
>>> usually created by P?S/8 PAL or perhaps converted binary paper-tapes in BIN 
>>> [or RIM] format.  Note: The P?S/8 MAP command is a proper super-set of OS/8 
>>> BITMAP and is called using the program name thus the PROGRAM is NAMED MAP.  
>>> [Note: This convention was born nearly simultaneously in both P?S/8 and 
>>> COS-300.  Both are partially based [but quickly diverge] from the original 
>>> R-L Monitor.  COS-300 is a redo of the original DIBOL-8, which is literally 
>>> the R-L Monitor with the two-letter commands hacked up to match what would 
>>> be in COS-300. [but nothing else; R-L only had the RU command and the 
>>> program names were only the first two characters].  But these changes were 
>>> made in both systems so that it would become possible to have the SET 
>>> command, the PAL command, and the MAP command, etc. and there is no general 
>>> concept of a "base" layer for which you need a command-decoder and then a 
>>> facade of software talking to software to make it look the way it does, and 
>>> this applies identically to P?S/8.  Also:  DEC officially recommended to 
>>> NOT enable CCL on tape-based systems due to the overhead.  Some members of 
>>> this group can personally attest to this overhead on real-world hardware.
>>>
>>> The most important feature over OS/8 is that the System Programs 
>>> Directory allows any program to include internal overlays, a feature taken 
>>> available by such as P?S/8 PAL and P?S/8 FOCAL, each of which actually have 
>>> several each.
>>>
>>> P?S/8 does not have BASIC [it can be written] but the point is that OS/8 
>>> has to have a string of independent core-mage programs that must chain to 
>>> each other to create a coherent environment and it is quite stilted and 
>>> restrictive, and requires creation of intermediate files as a restriction.  
>>> A P?S/8 version would just use overlays.
>>>
>>
>> Presently, work is being done to restore the last known version of EDU25 
>> BASIC for OS/8; there are problems with lost portions of the source files;  
>> I am doing some of the restoration recently, but it is quite broken!
>>
>> The goal is to then excise out enough of the product to be useful with 
>> the current "basic" P?S/8.  This is the first of two candidates.
>>
>> The second is to modify the source code of 8K BASIC which is a very 
>> different implementation.  It has NEVER been interfaced to any 
>> storage-based operating system and there are problems even running it from 
>> OS/8.  It is postulated that it will likely require 12K-16K to work with 
>> P?S/8 but will not lose any functionality over the paper-tape version.  
>> Even theoretically, this is not possible with OS/8; however, OS/8 has more 
>> choices for BASIC, but none of them suggest suitability for the present 
>> basic P?S/8 implementation; of course the future SHELL-extended P?S/8 will 
>> embrace them all.
>>  
>>
>>>
>>> Since most programs are simpler, especially user-written ones, there 
>>> exists a mechanism to just create a temporary system program by loading 
>>> binary files and then starting the temporary core-image.  [Note: Temporary 
>>> does not necessarily mean creation each and every time; all of field 0 is 
>>> saved in a virtual image; if not disturbed, it is thus semi-permanent.  If 
>>> the program is created in part because of debugging with P?S/8 ODT, a 
>>> proper super-set of OS/8 ODT, it can then be BSAVEd into a string of output 
>>> files for use later, etc.
>>>
>>
>> This feature is useful to create the equivalent of a P?S/8 system program 
>> without having to know the details to actually implement it,  Many programs 
>> fall into this category but not all;  some needs the additional features 
>> discussed elsewhere in this post; none of them are available in the 
>> so-called temporary variant. 
>>
>>>
>>> 2) The Tiny File System [TFS].  This is where all user files are saved 
>>> [mostly, see below].  Editing takes a little getting used to unless you are 
>>> a user of BASIC in which case the learning curve is measured in seconds.  
>>> Because a program tends to stretch across a few files, the typical overhead 
>>> of conventional editing is avoided.  The classic editing paradigm is to 
>>> have at least 100% of the size of the file empty space available [and also 
>>> a safety factor.  Any edit, even if only changing one line demands the 
>>> processing of the entire source file.  With fixed-length TFS files, you can 
>>> access just the portion that needs to be edited, load it into the editing 
>>> buffer, make the change, save it back.  Small changes are made in literally 
>>> a couple seconds.  [Note: There is a price to be paid for this of course.  
>>> You are inherently running without a  backup, but for the more nervous 
>>> users, make a copy of everything periodically.  And for nitpickers, yes, 
>>> one file can overflow.  to fix that, you have to split the file into two 
>>> parts.  The editing commands handle this.  For the more nitpickier of you, 
>>> this process can eventually get in trouble after many edits.  There is an 
>>> answer for that as well, using extended-length files as intermediates.
>>>
>>> 3) There actually is an Extended-Length File System, but it is not 
>>> actually used [other than to set the maximum block allowed by the device.  
>>> For example, since TFS files are bounded, the last file on a DECtape must 
>>> occupy 2660-2667; blocks 2700 and 2701 are wasted. Note:  OS/8 does not use 
>>> blocks, it uses RECORDS of two blocks each and then sloppily uses the terms 
>>> interchangeably.  These are logical DECtape blocks regardless of the actual 
>>> device used.  [Note:  P?S/8 exists for RK8E, RX01, TC12 LINCtape, TD8E 
>>> DECtape, etc.  AT this time, release is just for TC08 DECtape; however, 
>>> extended-length DECtapes really exist that go out to block 3277, thus more 
>>> files and no waste.]
>>>
>>> The INTENTION is to support an extended-length file that could occupy 
>>> the entire space normally under the purview of the TFS directory, but 
>>> instead is a single file.  Due to a temporary restriction which is 
>>> documented, an extended-length file EQUIVALENT is obtained by using the TFS 
>>> file that is the highest one on the TFS director as printed out by the 
>>> CA{talog} command.  Thus, there is a danger of screwing up and accidentally 
>>> specifying a TFS file where there is really another file still higher on 
>>> the device which will likely be destroyed.  By pre-allocating files and 
>>> using proper naming convention, this can be mindless.
>>>
>>
>> This is perhaps overstated, but the temporary restriction is easily 
>> learned, and all of this becomes moot in the next release where the new 
>> structure implements an even better overall outcome.  This was not 
>> anticipated until relatively recently; the temporary nature unfortunately 
>> was largely forgotten until recently, but now it is an easy part of the 
>> implementation of the next release, which is largely otherwise a lot of 
>> cleanup of minor issues and handler improvements, all preliminary steps to 
